we begin with breaking news from  afghanistan where the northern city of  kunduz has fallen into taliban hands  after afghan forces failed to hold the  strategically important city  afghanistan's sixth largest city near  the border with tajikistan kunduz has  been the scene of fierce fighting the  taliban is reported to also be advancing  into the center of sharipul another  northern provincial capital  and for more i'm joined by journalist  ali latifi in kabul ali  what what do we know about the situation  in the north of afghanistan right now so  the north of afghanistan we're seeing  several points with intense fighting and  back and forth claims by the taliban  that they have taken over different  areas and really at this point the only  way we can confirm these things is by  speaking to people on the ground and  having them tell us that yes the taliban  are  within these government offices and that  they've raised their flags and that  they've  essentially you know made it into the  central parts of these cities  because the government  sorry is not giving any direct  confirmation uh about  uh what's been going on they have yet to  outright confirm that condos  in the south or santa pola or any of  these other places  have come under taliban control but what  we do see is that they will release  statements and videos saying that there  are clearance operations and other  operations for instance they just  released the ministry of defense a video  of a commando in kunduz saying that  they've been conducting  intense operations over the last 24  hours and that they've kept  the taliban fighters from allegedly  making it to central points in the city  which is something that locals are  refuting saying no they've actually made  it into places like the prison into  police headquarters and things like that  so it's a lot of back and forth at this  point  right so no outright confirmation yet  from the afghan government but yet how  significant are these gains for the  taliban  um  condos is definitely a very significant  gain if you look at sort of their  strategy over the last few weeks or the  last couple months they really try to  take one of the major cities of the  country so they tried hard for kandao  they tried hard for lashkar ganeman and  they tried hard for hirat they weren't  able to take any of those and the first  cities that they were able to take zara  and shibaran they're considered smaller  they're not necessarily provinces that  get a lot of attention even from the  government they've always sort of been  ignored but a province like condos you  know given its population give it get  strategic location and also the fact  that they had been able to take it uh  two times prior in 2015 and 2016  is you know to the taliban it's a good  propaganda point you know it it's  something that they can sell very easily  so kudos is the third provincial capital  to fall in the past three days are the  taliban advancing quicker than expected  after the withdrawal of foreign troops  it seems that way but but the one thing  we have to remember is that a lot of  these advances are eventually repelled  um so  you know if we see that in the next few  days shibaran zarage and kunduz come  back into taliban hands that will come  back to the question that we've had all  this time of how long can the taliban  hold control over an area yes they can  take it you know for instance when the  game was mainly about districts over you  know the last few weeks the districts  would be taken and then a few days or a  couple weeks later the government would  say we've retaken that district so now  the question will be  if districts were trading hands so so  quickly in the past how long can they  keep cities or can they actually  maintain control over these cities for  extended periods of time  ali thank you very much ali letifi  journalist in kabul thank you very much  you
 

